# zenresize with_mask cost on x86-64 — the answer INVERTS vs aarch64: an i16 mask path pays here

date:    2026-08-29
commit:  14e5515 (main)
host:    GitHub Actions `macos-26-intel` — Intel Core i7-8700B @ 3.20 GHz (Coffee Lake)
isa:     AVX2 present (BMI1 BMI2 RTM ADX RDSEED …), no AVX-512. Confirmed in
         mask_e2e_ladder_x86_2026-08-29.macos-x64.cpu.txt — the AVX2 i16 kernels
         really executed, which is the whole point of running this off-laptop.
rustc:   rustc 1.98.0 (88d9e12ae 2026-08-18)
build:   bench profile (release), NO -C target-cpu=native, runtime dispatch (incant!)
threads: single-threaded resize
simd:    bench-simd-competitors OFF
bench:   .github/workflows/bench-mask.yml (workflow_dispatch), run 33236822984
log:     mask_e2e_ladder_x86_2026-08-29.macos-x64.log  (total 40.9 s)
issue:   https://github.com/imazen/zenresize/issues/3

## Why this run exists

`mask_e2e_ladder_aarch64_2026-08-29.meta` measured the same ladder on an M4 Pro
and concluded an i16 mask path was not worth building. That conclusion was
explicitly scoped to aarch64, because no machine in the aarch64 half of the dev
fleet can execute the AVX2 i16 kernels — Rosetta exposes SSE4.2 but not
AVX2/FMA, so a local `--target x86_64-apple-darwin` build compiles the V3
kernels without ever running them. CI's x64 runners can.

## Result (in-run paired ratio, rounded_mask/F32 vs no_mask/I16Srgb)

Positive = the forced f32 fallback is slower, i.e. what an i16 mask path could
win. All nine scenarios verified to take I16Srgb / F32 respectively.

| scenario             | no_mask (I16Srgb) | rounded_mask (F32) | 95% CI x86-64    | 95% CI aarch64 (M4, run 2) |
|----------------------|-------------------|--------------------|------------------|----------------------------|
| 64x64 -> 32x32       | 71.2 µs           | 86.0 µs            | +21.8% .. +22.5% | +3.0% .. +4.3%             |
| 128x128 -> 64x64     | 177.0 µs          | 204.8 µs           | +14.3% .. +15.1% | +1.4% .. +5.3%             |
| 256x256 -> 128x128   | 509.1 µs          | 551.8 µs           | +7.6% .. +8.3%   | -0.7% .. +3.3%             |
| 512x512 -> 256x256   | 1.6 ms            | 1.7 ms             | +4.9% .. +5.6%   | -0.8% .. +2.8%             |
| 800x600 -> 400x300   | 2.8 ms            | 3.0 ms             | +5.4% .. +6.1%   | -0.6% .. +3.4%             |
| 1024x1024 -> 512x512 | 5.9 ms            | 6.0 ms             | +0.7% .. +2.0% * | -1.4% .. +1.8%             |
| 1440x1080 -> 720x540 | 8.3 ms            | 9.0 ms             | +7.8% .. +9.2%   | -2.0% .. +1.3%             |
| 4K -> 1080p          | 42.4 ms           | 49.4 ms            | +15.8% .. +17.0% | -16.3% .. -14.7%           |
| 1080p -> 4K up       | 56.7 ms           | 81.9 ms            | +43.6% .. +45.1% | -30.5% .. -28.5%           |

`*` the only x86 cell whose CI crosses zero.

## Conclusion: the #3 decision is TARGET-DEPENDENT, not a yes/no

On x86-64 the forced f32 fallback costs 5-22% at nearly every size and **+44% on
a masked 1080p -> 4K upscale** (56.7 -> 81.9 ms). On aarch64 the same fallback is
*free or better* above ~256x256 and 15-30% FASTER at 4K. The two architectures
disagree in sign at the two largest sizes, not merely in magnitude.

So:

- Building an unconditional i16 mask path would win 5-45% on x86 and regress
  aarch64 masked 4K work by 15-30%.
- Not building it leaves 5-45% on the floor for every x86 deployment.
- Therefore the i16-vs-f32 choice for masked resizes has to be target-aware, the
  same way kernel selection already is. That is a design decision (and a
  precision one — the i16 path is u8-premultiplied double rounding vs f32's
  single rounding), so it is escalated rather than assumed.

This also confirms the direction of CLAUDE.md's long-standing "i16 ~ 2x faster"
note as an x86 statement, and confirms that note does NOT transfer to aarch64.

## Bigger finding, beyond masks

`no_mask` here is plain I16Srgb and `rounded_mask` is plain F32 plus a mask whose
own cost is ~0 (measured on 2026-08-27: the mask is free on the f32 path). So
these columns are close to a straight I16Srgb-vs-F32 comparison of the whole
resize, and they say the *automatic path selection* — not just the mask path —
is architecture-sensitive: I16Srgb is the right default on x86 at every size,
and the wrong default on aarch64 above ~2 MP (F32 is 15-30% faster there).
`new_inner()` currently picks I16Srgb for sRGB 4ch on both. Worth its own issue.

## Coverage / caveats

- One x86 microarchitecture only (Coffee Lake, AVX2, no AVX-512). The 7950X has
  AVX-512 and a much newer core; direction is likely to hold, magnitude may not.
- Shared CI runner: absolute timings are not comparable to the M4 Pro figures.
  Only the in-run paired ratio is, which is what the table reports.
- The same dispatch run also has `ubuntu-latest` (second x64 point) and
  `ubuntu-24.04-arm` (aarch64 control, which should reproduce the M4 Pro sign)
  legs; both were still running when this record was committed. Add them here.
